        "title": "How skyscrapers work",
        "text": "A  Throughout the history of architecture, people have continually tried to build higher. Thousands of workers laboured on the pyramids of ancient Egypt, the cathedrals of Europe and countless other towers, all trying to create something awe-inspiring. Although today people build skyscrapers mainly because they are convenient, ego and grandeur still sometimes play an important part in the scale of the building, just as they did in earlier times.\n\nB  Until fairly recently, however, there was a limit to how high builders could go, and the main obstacle was the downward pull of gravity. To build upwards, you need more material at the bottom to support the combined weight of all the material above it. For example, if you make the base of a pyramid bigger, you can keep building it higher, but this becomes unworkable because the base would take up too much land. As a result, people did not construct many buildings of more than ten storeys.\n\nC  In the late 1800s, however, a number of advances and circumstances came together, and engineers were able to break through the upper limit. In the USA, the social circumstances that led to skyscrapers were the growing American cities, most notably Chicago. Businesses all wanted their offices near the centre of town, but there was not enough space. In these cities, architects needed a way to expand upwards rather than outwards. The main technological advance that made skyscrapers possible was the development of mass iron and steel production. New manufacturing processes made it possible to produce long beams of solid iron. Narrow, relatively light metal beams could support a great deal of weight while taking up very little space. Then, with the arrival of the Bessemer process, the first efficient method of mass-producing steel, architects moved away from iron to steel.\n\nD  The central support structure of a skyscraper is its steel skeleton. Metal beams are riveted end to end to form vertical columns. At each floor level, these vertical columns are connected to horizontal girder beams. Many buildings also have diagonal beams between the girders for extra support. In a typical skyscraper substructure, each vertical column sits on a spread footing. The column rests directly on a cast-iron plate, which sits on top of a grillage: basically a stack of horizontal steel beams laid side by side in two or more layers. The grillage rests on a thick concrete pad, which lies on the soil. Once the steel is in place, the whole structure is covered with concrete.\n\nE  Once a building has more than five or six floors, you need something to move people up through it quickly and efficiently. Skyscrapers would never have worked without the development of elevator technology. Ever since the first passenger elevator was installed in New York's Haughwout Department Store in 1857, elevator shafts have been a major part of skyscraper design.\n\nF  Experts disagree about how high we can really go in the near future. Some say we could build a mile-high (1,609 m) building with existing technology, while others say we would need to develop lighter, stronger materials before such buildings were possible."
